>> title: Fatayer
>> category: Dumplings & Rolls
>> tags: spinach, yeast, sumac, lebanese, oven, hand pie
>> counters: Shawarma Counter
>> aka: fatayer, fatayir, fitiyir, fatayer sabanekh, spinach fatayer, spinach pies, spinach triangles, sabanekh, Lebanese spinach pie
>> pairs-with: labneh, sumac-onions, pita-bread
>> servings: 8
>> time: 2 hr 45 min
>> step.1: knead 8 min with yogurt and oil
>> step.2: prove 1 1/2 hr until doubled
>> step.3: salt the spinach 20 min, wring it dry
>> step.4: dress with lemon, sumac and onion
>> step.5: fold into triangles, pinch hard
>> step.6: bake at 400°F 20 min

Knead @all-purpose flour{3%cups}(390 g), @instant yeast{1 1/2%tsp}(5 g), @whole-milk yogurt{1/3%cup}(80 g), @olive oil{1/4%cup}(60 mL), @fine sea salt{1 1/2%tsp}(9 g) and @warm water{3/4%cup}(180 mL) ~knead{8%min} to a soft, satiny dough. The yogurt and the extra oil are here for one reason: this dough gets folded into a sharp corner three times and a lean bread dough cracks along every one of them.

Prove @&(~1)dough{} covered ~prove{1 1/2%hr}, until doubled.

Salt @baby spinach{1%lb}(450 g; chopped) with @kosher salt{1%tsp} and ~drain{20%min}, then take it up in a cloth and wring it until almost nothing more comes out. A fatayer leaks along its seams if the spinach is wet, and it leaks in the oven where you cannot fix it.

Dress @&(~1)wrung spinach{} with @yellow onion{1%small}(diced fine), @lemon juice{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @ground sumac{1%Tbs}(7 g), @extra-virgin olive oil{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @pine nuts{2%Tbs} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp}. Sour is the point — sumac and lemon both, and more than feels sensible, because the dough around it is plain.

Roll @&(~3)proved dough{} into five-inch rounds, mound @&(~1)spinach filling{} in the centre of each, and fold three sides up over it into a triangle, pinching each seam hard from the point inward. Pinch dry, with no filling caught in the seam, or it will open.

Bake @&(~1)folded triangles{} brushed with @olive oil{1%Tbs}(15 mL) on a lined sheet in the #oven{} at 400°F (200°C) ~bake{20%min}, until the seams are gold and the bases are firm.
